# rules: swift-cleartext-http-url
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-319.swift
# from: CWE-319 (Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information), MASVS-NETWORK-1
#
# A phone is on somebody else's network almost all of the time, and every one
# of those networks can read a cleartext request. The half that gets forgotten
# is the other direction: whoever can read the request can rewrite the reply,
# so this is not only a disclosure, it is a way to feed the app whatever the
# network decides to say.
#
# The address is read where it is handed to something that builds a request,
# rather than at every `http://` in the file. That is what keeps the rule off
# the string constants that are not addresses anybody dials - an XML namespace
# is the one that would otherwise dominate, exactly as it does in Android,
# because `http://www.w3.org/...` is a name and not a place.
#
# Loopback is excluded because nobody else is on it. `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`
# and `[::1]` are how a Swift process says "this machine", and a simulator
# talking to a server on the same laptop is not on a network at all.
#
# An app that does this also carries an `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` in its
# Info.plist, because App Transport Security blocks cleartext by default. Both
# are worth reporting: the plist says the decision was made, and this says
# where it is spent.
#
# Every receiver hole here is written `$$$S` rather than `$S`, which in Swift
# is the difference between a rule that fires and one that mostly does not. A
# single hole in receiver position compiles to `target: (_ . (_) @S .)` - a
# node with exactly one child - and the only receiver that fits is a bare name
# the grammar read as a `user_type`. `self.session`, `a.b`, `c?.request` and
# `d!.request` all fail it, silently, and that is most of the Swift ever
# written. See GUIDE.md.

[ "URL(string: $U)", "URL(string: $U, $$$_)",
  "NSURL(string: $U)", "NSURL(string: $U, $$$_)",
  "URLComponents(string: $U)", "URLRequest(url: $U)",
  "$$$S.request($U)", "$$$S.request($U, $$$_)",
  "$$$B.baseURL($U)", "$$$B.url($U)" ] as $dials
| "^\"http://" as $cleartext
| "^\"http://(?:localhost|127\\.0\\.0\\.1|0\\.0\\.0\\.0|\\[::1\\])[:/\"]" as $thisMachine
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $dials) as $all
| ( $all | of($dials)
    | where_capture("U"; $cleartext)
    | where_capture_not("U"; $thisMachine)
    | focus("U") )
| finding("swift-cleartext-http-url";
    "$U goes out in the clear, so whoever runs the network reads this request "
    + "and chooses the reply")
| report
